      "writtenOn": "2024-10-02T11:45:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "In general, you don\u0027t want comments to reflect exactly what the code does because if the code changes (e.g. we add a new `Capability` and `COUNT` is now `4`) either the comment itself must be updated with the code (resulting in larger diffs i.e. highrr risk of conflicts when rebasing/merging) or we forget about it and end up with a stale comment that doesn\u0027t match what the code does.\n\nOn the phrasing itself, the comment shouldn\u0027t repeat what the code already says; `assert_eq` clearly expresses that we are \"verifying something\" and `Capability::COUNT` that that _something_ is the number of know capabilities. Instead, the comment should tell us _why_ we\u0027re doing this.\n\nRegarding Rust, regular comments use `//` as `///` are reserved for [doc comments](https://doc.rust-lang.org/reference/comments.html#doc-comments).\n\nSo, all-in-all, a more generic yet informative comment could be\n```\n// Fail if this test doesn\u0027t actually cover all supported capabilities.\n```",
